.TH "MYD" "1" "August 2026" "myd 0.43.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
myd \- vi-like terminal file browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmyd\fR [\fB\-2\fR] [\fB\-s\fR] [\fIPATH\fR [\fIRIGHT\fR]]
.br
\fBmyd\fR [\fB\-s\fR] \fB\-d\fR
.br
\fBmyd\fR [\fIPATH\fR] \fBsftp://\fR[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR][:\fIpath\fR|/\fIpath\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmyd\fR is a terminal user interface (TUI) for browsing and managing the
filesystem. It provides a directory tree with \fBvi\fR-style key bindings,
a live info panel showing file metadata, and proportional size bars that
visualize disk usage at a glance.
.PP
Built with \fBratatui\fR and \fBcrossterm\fR.
.PP
If \fIPATH\fR is provided, the browser starts at that directory; otherwise it
starts in the current directory. The picker is reached with \fBg d\fR, or at
start-up with \fB\-\-directory\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-2, \-\-dual
Start in dual-panel mode, with two independent views side by side. Implied by
passing a second path.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-directory
Start on the picker and choose from your saved directories and hosts, rather
than opening a directory \(em the same list \fBg d\fR opens, which the letter
matches. Cannot be combined with \fIPATH\fR, which would be supplying the
destination the flag exists to ask for.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-shallow
Browse without measuring directory sizes, as though \fBS\fR had been pressed on
arrival. Applies to both panes of a split. Useful over a network mount or a
large archive, where the recursive walk is the slowest part of opening a
directory and is rarely worth waiting for just to look around.
.IP
Combines with \fB\-d\fR: the flag describes the session, so it carries through
the picker to whichever directory you choose from it. The picker's path field
names the mode it will use \(em \fBshallow\fR or \fBfull\fR \(em so which way a
path will open is visible before \fBEnter\fR. A directory with a remembered
preference of its own keeps it; the flag supplies the default for the rest.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.SS Navigation
.TP
.B j
Move cursor down one line.
.TP
.B k
Move cursor up one line.
.TP
.B h
In the tree view, collapse the current directory or move the cursor to the
parent entry. In the treemap view, move the cursor one tile to the left; when
already on a left-edge tile, step up to the parent directory.
.IP
On the root line, \fBh\fR goes up out of the directory the pane was opened at:
back to the previous view if you arrived by descending, otherwise re-rooting the
pane one level higher, up to \fB/\fR. A pane opened deep in a tree \(em a remote
connected at \fBsftp://host:dir\fR, or a path given on the command line \(em is
therefore not confined to it. A remote re-roots over the same connection.
.TP
.B l
In the tree view, expand the current directory. In the treemap view, move the
cursor one tile to the right.
.TP
.B gg
Jump to the first line of the tree.
.TP
.B G
Jump to the last line of the tree.
.TP
.B Ctrl+f
Move down a full screen. The PageDown key does the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+b
Move up a full screen. The PageUp key does the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+d
Move down half a screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl+u
Move up half a screen.
.TP
.B g
Start a two-key chord and wait for the second key, however long that takes \(em
there is no timeout, so the sequence is decided by what you type rather than how
fast. While it waits, the footer shows \fBg…\fR followed by the keys that
complete it (\fBg\fR, \fBu\fR, \fBd\fR, \fBs\fR, \fBr\fR, \fBx\fR) in place of
the ordinary bindings, which are not live until the chord resolves. \fBEsc\fR
abandons it; any other key rings the bell rather than acting on its own.
.TP
.B g u
Move cursor to the parent directory.
.TP
.B g d
Prompt for a new root directory and navigate to it. The picker opens over the
current view; \fBEsc\fR dismisses it and returns to where you were.
.IP
The picker lists local directories and saved remote hosts together, in two
labelled sections \(em both answer "where do you want to go", and keeping two
near-identical screens meant two places to fix every bug. \fBEnter\fR opens a
directory or connects to a host, whichever the highlighted row is.
.IP
It has three panes: a path field, the list below it, and a panel of actions to
the right of the list. \fBTab\fR cycles them in that order \(em you pick what to
act on, then what to do to it \(em and the focused one is outlined in cyan.
.IP
With the field focused, type a path (\fB~\fR is expanded) and press \fBEnter\fR.
With the list focused, the \fBarrow keys\fR move through the entries. The arrows
reach the list from the field too: the first press selects the entry already
highlighted rather than stepping past it, and further presses move.
\fBLeft\fR/\fBRight\fR hand the keyboard back to the field, as do \fBHome\fR and
\fBEnd\fR when no search is running.
.IP
Selecting an entry offers its path in the field, and typing there replaces that
offer \(em press \fBEnd\fR first to extend it instead, which is how you append
a subdirectory to a listed path.
.IP
Every directory opened through this dialog is remembered, whether typed or
chosen from the list, so a path typed once is a keystroke away afterwards.
Directories reached by browsing the tree are not: the list is for destinations
you chose, and recording every directory drilled into would bury them.
.IP
With the list focused, every printable key searches it: typing narrows the list
as you go, with no \fB/\fR to press first and no letters held back for commands.
\fBBackspace\fR unwinds the search a character at a time, and past the start
hands the keyboard back to the path field. \fBEsc\fR clears the filter; a second
\fBEsc\fR, with nothing left to clear, closes the picker. While a search is
running, \fBHome\fR and \fBEnd\fR jump to the ends of the narrowed list, and a
search that leaves a single row opens it on \fBEnter\fR. Searching does not
disturb the path field, which stays empty so \fBEnter\fR acts on the row you
narrowed to.
.IP
The actions panel offers what can be done to the highlighted entry: go or
connect, save a directory, edit, forget, pin or unpin, move, and turn measuring
on or off. Only the operations that apply to that row are listed \(em a saved
host has no pin or measuring toggle, and a row that is not saved has nothing to
forget \(em so the panel never shows a choice that would do nothing. \fBj\fR /
\fBk\fR or the \fBarrow keys\fR walk it and \fBEnter\fR runs the highlighted
action.
.IP
Each action also carries a letter, shown picked out in magenta, that runs it
outright without walking to it: \fBg\fR go or connect, \fBs\fR save, \fBe\fR
edit, \fBf\fR forget, \fBp\fR pin, \fBu\fR unpin, \fBm\fR move, \fBd\fR toggle
measuring. Case does not matter. A letter the highlighted row does not offer
does nothing rather than running whatever sits in its place, so \fBp\fR on a
saved host is inert. Most are the label's own initial; \fBg\fR and \fBd\fR are
not, because those two labels change with the row \(em "Go" becomes "Connect"
on a host, and the measuring toggle reads "Skip measuring" or "Measure sizes"
depending on which way it currently sits \(em and a shortcut that moved with the
label would be a trap.
.IP
These operations were single letters bound on the list until version 0.33, which
is what made the list unsearchable: \fBd\fR in "downloads" forgot an entry
rather than filtering. Moving them into their own pane freed every printable key
for the search.
.IP
Saving prompts for a destination to keep \(em a path, or a \fBlabel = sftp://…\fR
line to save a host. Editing opens the highlighted entry \(em a directory's path,
or a host's details \(em in a popup, keeping its visit history and its place in
the pinned block rather than making a correction mean deleting and re-adding.
The measuring toggle applies to the highlighted directory without opening it
\(em marking somewhere known to be slow should not require paying for the walk
first \(em and the row shows \fB(shallow)\fR when measuring is off.
.IP
The path field's title names the traversal the destination will get \(em
\fBshallow\fR or \fBfull\fR \(em resolved exactly as opening it will resolve it:
the directory's own remembered preference when it has one, otherwise the
session default that \fB\-s\fR sets. Which way a path will open is therefore
visible before \fBEnter\fR rather than inferred afterwards from whether sizes
appeared. A remote target is labelled with neither, since it is connected to
rather than walked.
.IP
Remembered directories are stored in the \fB[[favorite]]\fR section of
\fB~/.config/myd/hosts.toml\fR beside the saved hosts, and shown in three
groups: pinned entries (\fB\[u25B2]\fR) at the top in an order you arrange,
saved entries (\fB\[u2605]\fR) next, then automatically remembered ones.
.IP
A catalog with no directories in it \(em a first run, or a file that only ever
held hosts \(em is seeded with the usual locations (home, Desktop, Documents,
Downloads, Pictures, Music, Videos, / and /tmp) and written out. They are
ordinary entries from then on: pin, reorder and delete them like any other. A
file that already lists directories is left alone, and a file that does not
parse is never written over. Within the last two groups the order is most-recently-visited first,
so the places you actually use rise to the top.
.IP
\fBPin\fR puts the highlighted entry at the \fIbottom\fR of the pinned block
\(em the block is an order you arranged, so a new pin does not barge into the
middle of it \(em and \fBUnpin\fR takes it out again, keeping the entry.
\fBMove\fR starts a reorder, handing the keyboard back to the list:
\fBj\fR/\fBk\fR (or the arrows) reposition the entry, \fBEnter\fR confirms and
\fBEsc\fR puts it back where it started. \fBMove\fR on an entry that is not yet
pinned pins it first, so positioning something never needs two steps.
.IP
Sliding an entry one step past the bottom of the block takes it out: the row's
marker changes as it leaves, so what \fBEnter\fR will do is visible before it
is committed, and \fBk\fR brings it back in.
.IP
Automatically remembered directories are trimmed to the most recent twenty.
Anything explicitly saved or pinned is never trimmed.
.TP
.B 0
Collapse all expanded directories.
.TP
.B *
Expand all directories.
.SS Tagging
Most operations act on the file under the cursor. To act on several at once,
tag them first: \fBc\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBD\fR and \fBg r\fR all take the tagged set
when there is one, and fall back to the single selection when there is not.
.TP
.B t
Tag or untag the current selection. Works in both views \(em the tree row under
the cursor, or the selected treemap tile \(em and the two share one set of tags,
so switching views with \fBv\fR never changes what is tagged. A tagged tree row
is highlighted; a tagged tile is marked \fB\[u25B6]\fR and outlined in green.
.TP
.B V
Visual mode: tag a range as the cursor moves. Every row swept between the anchor
and the cursor is tagged; leaving visual mode keeps them, so ranges elsewhere in
the tree can be added the same way. Tree view only \(em a range is a span of
consecutive rows, and the treemap's tiles have no such order \(em pressing it in
the treemap says so rather than doing nothing.
.TP
.B U
Remove every tag.
.SS File Operations
.TP
.B D
Delete the tagged files, or the selected one when nothing is tagged (with a
confirmation dialog). Directories are removed recursively.
.IP
The confirmation takes \fBy\fR, \fBn\fR, or \fBa\fR \(em delete and stop asking
for the rest of the session.
.TP
.B R
Rename the currently selected file or directory. An existing name is never
overwritten.
.TP
.B g r
Rename every tagged file by regular expression \(em the same operation as
\fBR\fR, applied across the tagged set. The dialog takes a pattern and a
replacement, in which \fB$1\fR is the first capture group; \fBTab\fR moves
between the two fields. The preview shows the first tagged file transformed, so
the pattern can be checked before it is applied. Files the pattern does not
match are skipped rather than treated as failures, since tagging a mixed set and
renaming only part of it is the ordinary case.
.TP
.B N
Create a new directory in the current location.
.TP
.B c
Copy the tagged files, or the selected one, into the other panel's directory.
Each name already taken at the destination is confirmed individually, whether
the copy is local or runs through the transfer queue; declining skips that file
and leaves the existing one alone. In that prompt, and in every dialog,
\fBTab\fR moves between the buttons and never answers \(em only \fBEnter\fR, a
click on a button, or the button's own letter decides. \fBEsc\fR cancels.
.TP
.B m
Move the tagged files, or the selected one, into the other panel's directory.
Within a single filesystem this is a rename, so it completes instantly whatever
the size; between two different hosts the bytes are copied and the source is
removed only after that file's copy succeeds. A cross-host move is queued and
reported in the transfer panel exactly like a copy.
.IP
When a name is already taken at the destination, myd asks what to do: overwrite
it, skip that file and continue, or cancel the whole move. Cancelling abandons
every remaining file, including ones that would not have collided.
.TP
.B r
Rescan: clear the size cache and re-read sizes from disk. Sizes are otherwise
cached and reused as you navigate, so this is how to pick up on-disk changes.
.SS View
.TP
.B v
Toggle between the tree view and the treemap view, carrying the selection over
so the entry you were looking at in one is the one highlighted in the other. The
chosen view persists as you navigate between directories.
.IP
Either view fills the panel and carries the panel's border \(em bright cyan
when it holds the keyboard, grey otherwise \(em so a split reads the same
whichever view each side is showing. The treemap's box is titled with the
directory it covers and how to get back to the tree.
.IP
\fBEnter\fR opens the selected directory in either view. Treemap tiles for
directories end in a slash, as in
.BR ls (1),
so a tile says whether there is anything to open.
.TP
.B Ctrl+p
Toggle the info panel sidebar. Hidden by default. \fBTab\fR moves focus into
it, where \fBj\fR/\fBk\fR walk the editable fields, \fBEnter\fR edits one, and
\fB<\fR/\fB>\fR resize the panel. See
.B INFO PANEL
below.
.TP
.B Ctrl+l
Redraw the whole screen, as in a shell or
.BR vi (1).
Only the cells that change are normally rewritten, so a display corrupted by
something outside \fBmyd\fR's control \(em a stray write from another process, a
resize the terminal reported oddly \(em stays wrong until something happens to
overwrite each damaged cell. This repaints everything.
.TP
.B space
Open or close the file preview pane, which covers most of the screen and shows
the contents of the selected file. Opening it also gives it focus, so the vi
motions act on the pane rather than on the tree behind it. On a multi-page PDF the
motions turn pages. On an archive the pane lists what is inside it. See
.B FILE PREVIEW
and
.B ARCHIVES
below.
.TP
.B s
Cycle through sort modes: largest, smallest, dirs-first, files-first, newest
(modification time), oldest (modification time), and recently accessed (access
time).
.TP
.B 1 \- 8
Sort by that entry of the sort menu, without opening it: \fB5\fR is "sort by
newest". The digits are the menu's own numbering, so anything the menu offers
has a one-key shortcut. \fB0\fR is unrelated and still collapses everything; the
menu starts at 1.
.TP
.B g s
Open a numbered menu of the sort orders and pick one outright, rather than
cycling to it. The number chooses directly, so \fBg s 5\fR does what the bare
\fB5\fR does; \fBj\fR and \fBk\fR move through the menu and \fBEnter\fR
accepts. Clicking the \fBSort:\fR indicator in the status bar opens the same
menu. \fBs\fR remains quicker once the order is known \(em this is for going
straight to one, and for discovering what the orders are.
.TP
.B H
Toggle visibility of hidden (dot) files and directories.
.TP
.B B
Toggle display of proportional size bars in the tree.
.TP
.B P
Toggle an
.BR ls (1)-style
permissions column, shown to the left of each entry's name. Entries whose
backend does not report permissions show
.BR ?--------- .
.TP
.B T
Toggle a modification-time column, shown to the left of each entry's name.
Entries with no reported time show a dash.
.TP
.B /
Search for a file or directory by name. The pattern is a case-insensitive
regular expression; \fBn\fR and \fBp\fR step to the next and previous match,
wrapping at the ends.
.TP
.B f
Filter the tree by a case-insensitive regular expression: entries whose names
do not match are hidden, at every level. A directory is kept when something
beneath it matches, so matching files stay reachable. The filter is a view mask
\(em the tree itself is untouched \(em and an empty pattern clears it.
.IP
While a filter is active the title bar reads \fBFILTERED\fR and reports how
many entries are \fIshown\fR rather than how many exist, and the footer carries
the pattern \(em a masked view is never mistaken for the directory's real
contents. On a narrow terminal the footer badge drops its hint, then the
pattern, but never the fact that filtering is on.
.IP
A pattern that is not valid regular expression syntax is reported. Note that
\fB*p$\fR is not valid (there is nothing for \fB*\fR to repeat); the pattern
for "names ending in p" is \fBp$\fR, or \fB.*p$\fR.
.TP
.B ? / F1
Show or hide the key reference. It is taller than a typical terminal, so
\fBj\fR / \fBk\fR, the arrow keys, PageUp/PageDown, \fBg\fR / \fBG\fR and the
mouse wheel scroll it; the title bar shows your position in the list.
.SS Screen Level
.TP
.B q
Back out of the current state, or quit when there is none. One thing at a time,
innermost first: a running scan is cancelled, the \fBg d\fR picker is closed, an
active filter is cleared, and an archive is left \(em so a filter set inside an
archive takes two presses, one for each. Everywhere else the key means "back out
of this", and ending the whole session instead was a surprise.
.IP
With nothing left to undo it quits. Transfers still queued or running are
confirmed first, since abandoning one discards a partial copy. \fBCtrl+C\fR
force-quits without asking.
.TP
.B Esc
The same as \fBq\fR, including the confirmation.
.TP
.B Ctrl+o
Go back to the parent directory.
.TP
.B o
Open the selected file or directory with the system's default application \(em
.BR open (1)
on macOS,
.BR xdg\-open (1)
elsewhere. The launcher is started and myd carries on; it is not waited for, and
its output is discarded so it cannot corrupt the display.
.IP
Remote entries are refused: those launchers only understand local paths, and a
remote path either fails or opens an unrelated local file that happens to share
it. Copy the file across first with \fBc\fR.
.TP
.B O
Open with a program you name. A dialog asks for a command line; the selection is
appended to it and the result is run.
.IP
The command is split on whitespace, honouring single and double quotes so a
program under a path with a space in it can still be named. It is
.I not
passed to a shell, so there is no expansion, no globbing and no \fB$VAR\fR \(em
a filename can never be read as syntax. A bare name is looked up on \fBPATH\fR;
anything containing a
.B /
is used as written.
.IP
The files come after your own arguments, as absolute paths: with two files
tagged, \fBvim \-v\fR runs \fBvim \-v /abs/one /abs/two\fR. As everywhere else,
the tagged files are the target when there are any, and the selected one
otherwise.
.IP
myd leaves the alternate screen while the program runs and takes it back when it
exits, so a full\-screen editor or pager behaves exactly as it would from the
shell. A non\-zero exit status is reported, since it would otherwise vanish with
the screen.
.IP
The dialog's buttons are \fBOK\fR and \fBCancel\fR. \fBTab\fR reaches them and
never presses one; \fBEnter\fR in the command field is the same as \fBOK\fR.
.IP
Refused inside an archive and on remote panels, for the same reason \fBo\fR is:
there is no path on this machine to give the program.
.TP
.B S
Browse without measuring directory sizes, and back again.
.IP
Measuring means a recursive walk, which is the slowest thing this program does;
over a large archive or a network mount it is not worth waiting for merely to
look around. In shallow mode directory sizes read as unknown \(em a dash and an
empty bar, exactly as remote directories do \(em and sort last rather than
masquerading as small. The title bar reads \fBSHALLOW\fR so a tree of dashes is
never mistaken for a broken one.
.IP
Turning measuring back on asks first, since that is the walk you avoided. The
choice is remembered per directory in \fB~/.config/myd/hosts.toml\fR, so
somewhere you have decided not to measure opens that way next time. It can also
be set from the \fBgd\fR picker's actions panel, without opening the directory
at all.
.IP
A pane that is already shallow stays shallow as you walk into subdirectories,
whatever those directories have remembered: what the pane shows is what it does,
and a walk you can see it avoiding should not start because of a decision made
in an earlier session. A remembered preference still applies when you arrive
from a measuring pane, and \fBS\fR measures the directory you are in whenever
you want it. This matters most after climbing out of a tree with \fBh\fR: the
directory you step back into may well be one you once browsed in full.
.TP
.B Ctrl+r
Rescan the file tree.
.TP
.B Ctrl+p
Toggle the info panel sidebar.
.TP
.B gd
Open the picker: a path field, your saved directories and remote hosts in one
list below it, and a panel of actions to the right of that list. \fBTab\fR
cycles the three in that order and the focused one is outlined in cyan.
.IP
In the list, the \fBarrow keys\fR navigate and \fBEnter\fR opens a directory or
connects to a host depending on which kind the cursor is on. Every other
printable key searches: typing narrows the list as you go, with no \fB/\fR to
press first. \fBBackspace\fR unwinds the search a character at a time and
\fBEsc\fR clears it \(em a second \fBEsc\fR, with nothing left to clear, closes
the picker. While a search is running, \fBHome\fR and \fBEnd\fR jump to the ends
of the narrowed list. If the search leaves a single row, \fBEnter\fR opens it
straight away.
.IP
The actions panel lists what can be done to the highlighted entry \(em go or
connect, save a directory, edit, forget, pin or unpin, move, and toggle
measuring \(em showing only those that apply to that row, so a saved host offers
no pin and a built-in location offers no forget. \fBj\fR / \fBk\fR or the
\fBarrow keys\fR walk the panel and \fBEnter\fR runs the highlighted action.
Each one also has a letter, picked out in magenta, that runs it directly:
\fBg\fR, \fBs\fR, \fBe\fR, \fBf\fR, \fBp\fR, \fBu\fR, \fBm\fR and \fBd\fR.
These operations used to be single letters bound on the list itself, which is
what made the list unsearchable by name.
.IP
The path field takes a local path or a remote
\fBsftp://[user@]host[:port][:path|/path]\fR URL, so an address you have not
saved needs no separate step \(em type it and press \fBEnter\fR. A connection
replaces whatever the active panel was showing.
.IP
The starting directory may be given after a \fB/\fR or, as \fBscp\fR writes it,
after a \fB:\fR. The two differ in what they are relative to: \fBhost:work\fR
starts in \fIwork\fR under the login directory, while \fBhost:/work\fR and
\fBhost/work\fR name \fI/work\fR itself. A relative path is resolved by the
server on connection, so it follows the account's home wherever that is.
.TP
.B Ctrl+N
Release the mouse so the terminal's own text selection works; press again to
re-grab it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+t
Show or hide the transfer panel.
.TP
.B Tab
Rotates through every visible pane in turn \(em each browser panel from left to
right, each followed immediately by its own info panel when that is shown, then
the transfer panel \(em and wraps back to the first. Only one pane shows the
bright focus border at a time, and the footer switches to describe whichever
pane that is. Focusing an info panel makes the panel it belongs to the active
one, since the fields it edits are that panel's selection. While the transfer
panel has focus, \fBj\fR / \fBk\fR move between transfers, \fBK\fR,
\fBDelete\fR or \fBBackspace\fR cancels the selected one (after a
confirmation), \fBC\fR clears the finished ones, and \fBEsc\fR returns to the
tree. A double-click on a transfer also cancels it.
.IP
\fBDelete\fR and \fBBackspace\fR both cancel because which of the two a
terminal sends for the key labelled "delete" depends on the terminal and its
configuration \(em on a Mac keyboard the obvious key sends \fBBackspace\fR.
Neither does anything else in this pane.
.IP
Transfers that have stopped are collected under one \fBFinished\fR heading that
names each outcome it contains \(em "1 done, 2 aborted", say. A completed
transfer is marked \fB\[u2713]\fR in green, a failure \fB!\fR in red with its
error beneath it, and one you cancelled \fB\[u2A2F]\fR in yellow, labelled
\fBaborted\fR. Until version 0.35.3 the heading read "Done", which counted a
transfer you had just killed among the completions.
.IP
The footer describes whichever pane holds the keyboard, and only that one \(em
there is one keyboard, so exactly one set of keys is ever on screen. In a split
the unfocused panel draws no footer at all, and the focused one draws its keys
across the whole width. Two panels each describing themselves would put, say,
\fB[TREE]\fR and \fB[TREEMAP]\fR side by side, which not only reads as two live
keymaps but as two contradictory ones: \fBj\fR / \fBk\fR and \fBhjkl\fR mean
different things in the two views.
.IP
Movement is plain \fBj\fR / \fBk\fR, as everywhere else in the app, and both
keys that destroy something are shifted. Until version 0.35.1 this was reversed
\(em \fBk\fR cancelled and \fBK\fR moved up \(em which put the one key a vi user
presses without thinking on the destructive action.
.IP
\fBC\fR drops only entries that have stopped \(em completed, failed and
cancelled \(em and never touches anything queued or in flight, so it cannot
abandon work in progress. It asks for no confirmation, unlike cancelling:
what it discards is a record of work already done, not the work itself. The
selection is dropped along with the row it was on, so the next \fBK\fR cannot
land on a transfer that merely slid up into that slot.
.TP
.B gx
Cancel all queued and in-flight transfers.
.SH REMOTE BROWSING
myd can browse and transfer files over SFTP. Connect from the \fBgd\fR picker
\(em pick a saved host, or type an \fBsftp://\fR URL into its path field \(em or
launch with a remote target:
.PP
.RS
.nf
myd sftp://prod
myd sftp://user@host:2222/var/log
myd sftp://user@host:work/notes
myd /tmp sftp://prod
.fi
.RE
.PP
Either positional argument may be the remote one. Giving a local path and a
remote target together opens them side by side, ready to copy between; a remote
target on its own puts the current directory in the other pane.
.PP
Authentication reuses your existing SSH setup: the ssh-agent, then keys under
\fB~/.ssh\fR (prompting for a passphrase if a key is encrypted), then a password
as a last resort. \fB~/.ssh/config\fR host aliases and \fB~/.ssh/known_hosts\fR
are honored. Credentials are never written to disk.
.PP
Copying (\fBc\fR) between a remote and a local panel runs as a background
transfer rather than blocking the interface. Up to sixteen transfers run at once
and further requests stack up. The transfer panel appears once you start a copy
(toggle it any time with \fBCtrl+t\fR) and shows each transfer's progress, rate,
and ETA. Large files download through many concurrent pipelined reads, and the
files and subdirectories within a directory copy transfer concurrently, so a
tree of small files is not paced by round-trip latency.
.PP
Creating directories (\fBN\fR), renaming (\fBR\fR), deleting (\fBD\fR) and
moving (\fBm\fR) are all routed through the backend the panel is browsing, so
they act on the remote host rather than the local machine.
.PP
Remote directory sizes cannot be measured cheaply: a \fBdu\fR-style walk over
SFTP costs one round trip per directory. Rather than report the directory
inode's own size as though it were a total, such directories show a dash
(\(em) with an empty size bar, and they sort as \fIunknown\fR \(em grouped
last \(em rather than as small. This holds whether or not the directory is
expanded: expanding reveals one level, not the whole subtree, so a running
total would grow with each expansion and never be the truth. The treemap
likewise gives unmeasured directories equal tiles instead of implying a size
comparison it cannot make.
.PP
The info panel shows only what the directory listing carries (size and
timestamps); owner, group and creation time are not part of it.
.SH SAVED HOSTS
Saved remote locations live in \fB~/.config/myd/hosts.toml\fR (honouring
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR) and are hand-editable. An entry records a label, host,
optional user, port and starting path, plus a use count and the time of the last
connection.
.PP
Hosts are listed after the directories in the \fBgd\fR picker, ordered strictly
by recency \(em most recently connected first \(em so the list changes visibly
as you work. The use count is displayed but does not affect the order.
.PP
.B No passwords are stored.
Authentication goes through the same ladder as always: ssh-agent, then
\fB~/.ssh\fR keys, then a prompt.
.PP
A malformed file is reported and ignored rather than overwritten, so a typo can
be corrected by hand instead of costing the whole list.
.SH MOUSE
The wheel scrolls the focused view; a left click focuses a panel and selects a
row or treemap tile; a right click selects and opens. Mouse capture takes over
the terminal's own click-drag selection, so \fBCtrl+N\fR releases and re-grabs
it. Most terminals also honour Shift+drag while captured.
.PP
With the preview open, a left click advances it as \fBj\fR does \(em turning the
page of a document, or stepping to the next file when an image is on screen. A
click\-drag is ignored: the gesture is decided when the button comes up, so
dragging across a picture does not page past it.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Setting \fBMYD_TRACE=1\fR writes a log to \fB~/.cache/myd\-trace.log\fR (override
with \fBMYD_TRACE_FILE\fR). \fBMYD_LOG\fR takes a filter directive instead, e.g.
\fBMYD_LOG=myd::transfer=debug\fR for just the transfer engine;
\fBMYD_LOG_FORMAT=json\fR makes the output machine-readable.
.PP
The log records key and event-loop timings, connection setup, the SFTP read
limit the server negotiated, and one line per transferred file with the path
taken, chunk size, window depth and achieved rate. Per-chunk latencies are
accumulated into a histogram and summarised once per file, so tracing never
becomes the bottleneck it is measuring. It is off by default and costs nothing
when unset.
.SH TUNING TRANSFERS
Every performance-relevant setting is an environment variable, so a slow link
can be diagnosed without a rebuild.
.TP
.B MYD_SSH_WINDOW
SSH channel window in bytes (default 64 MiB). This is the hard ceiling on
in-flight data: throughput cannot exceed \fBwindow / rtt\fR however deep the
request pipeline is.
.TP
.B MYD_SSH_NODELAY
Disable Nagle's algorithm on the SSH socket (default true).
.TP
.B MYD_SSH_MAX_PACKET
SSH maximum packet size in bytes (default 32768, \fBrussh\fR's own default).
Not a bottleneck, and exposed only so it can be ruled out \(em raising it risks
a disconnect from strict servers.
.TP
.B MYD_SFTP_WRITE_LIMIT
Outstanding bytes on the sequential write path (default 16 MiB).
.TP
.B MYD_SFTP_MAX_PENDING
SFTP requests in flight on one connection (default 1024).
.TP
.B MYD_CHUNK_SIZE
Bytes per chunk (default 256 KiB). On the parallel path one chunk is one wire
request, so a value above the server's negotiated read limit becomes several
serial requests rather than one larger one.
.TP
.B MYD_CHUNKS_IN_FLIGHT
Concurrent chunk reads within one large file (default 32).
.TP
.B MYD_MAX_PARALLEL
Concurrent transfers, and files per directory level (default 16).
.TP
.B MYD_GLOBAL_CONCURRENCY
Ceiling on concurrent operations during a recursive copy (default 192). Without
it the per-level windows multiply.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Besides the transfer tunables above, myd reads:
.TP
.B PATH
Searched to resolve the program named in the \fBO\fR dialog, when that program
is given as a bare name rather than a path.
.TP
.B MYD_PREFS
Names the preferences file outright, ahead of the usual lookup. Preferences
otherwise live in \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/myd/prefs.toml\fR, falling back to
\fB~/.config/myd/prefs.toml\fR. The file is written only on exit, and only when
a preference has actually changed; a missing or malformed one costs the
settings rather than the session, and is left alone so it can be repaired by
hand.
.SH SORT MODES
Press \fBs\fR to cycle through the available sort modes, or type the mode's
number (\fB1\fR\-\fB8\fR, as listed in the \fBg s\fR menu) to go straight to it:
.TP
.B 1 largest
Items sorted by descending size. Directories are measured recursively.
.TP
.B 2 smallest
Items sorted by ascending size. Directories are measured recursively.
.TP
.B 3 dirs-first
Directories are listed before files; alphabetical order within each group.
.TP
.B 4 files-first
Files are listed before directories; alphabetical order within each group.
.TP
.B 5 newest
Items sorted by modification time, most recent first.
.TP
.B 6 oldest
Items sorted by modification time, least recent first.
.TP
.B 7 recently-accessed
Items sorted by access time, most recent first.
.TP
.B 8 natural
By name, with runs of digits compared as numbers, so \fBfile2\fR sorts before
\fBfile10\fR.
.PP
The time-based sorts use the timestamps from the directory listing, so they work
identically on local and remote (SFTP) trees with no extra latency.
.SH SYMLINKS
Symbolic links are shown with a distinct link icon, a cyan italic name, and a
trailing \fB@\fR (\fB@/\fR when the link points at a directory), so they remain
distinguishable on terminals without color.
.PP
A symlink to a directory can be entered and expanded exactly like a real
directory, on both local and remote (SFTP) trees. Sizes and timestamps shown for
a link are those of its target. A broken link keeps the values reported by the
directory listing.
.SH SIZE BARS
Each entry in the tree displays a proportional size bar indicating the item's
share of its parent directory's total content. The bar is color-coded:
.TP
.B Green
The item occupies less than 50% of its parent's total size.
.TP
.B Amber
The item occupies 50\-80% of its parent's total size.
.TP
.B Red
The item occupies more than 80% of its parent's total size.
.PP
Toggle the size bars on and off with \fBb\fR.
.SH TREEMAP VIEW
Press \fBv\fR to switch between the file tree and a squarified treemap that
visualizes disk usage as proportional rectangular tiles: larger files and
directories occupy more area. Move between tiles with \fBh\fR, \fBj\fR,
\fBk\fR, and \fBl\fR; press \fBl\fR or \fBEnter\fR to descend into a directory.
.PP
Each tile is filled with a color for its content category: code, docs, images,
video, audio, archives, data, binaries, or other. A file's category comes from
its extension; a directory takes the category holding most of its bytes. A
legend in the status bar names the categories currently on screen. When a tile
is too narrow to show its full name, the selected item's name is shown in the
status bar.
.PP
Tiles can be tagged with \fBt\fR just as tree rows can, and the two views share
one set of tags \(em switching with \fBv\fR never changes what is tagged, and
\fBc\fR, \fBm\fR and \fBD\fR act on the same set from either. A tagged tile
carries the \fB\[u25B6]\fR marker and a green outline, so it reads as tagged even
when it is too small for a label. \fBV\fR is the exception: a visual range is a
span of consecutive rows and the treemap has no such order, so it applies in the
tree view only and says so if pressed here.
.SH INFO PANEL
The right-hand sidebar (hidden by default; toggle with \fBCtrl+p\fR)
describes the selected item, one field per row:
.TP
.B Name
The file or directory name.
.TP
.B Type
File, Directory, or Symlink.
.TP
.B Size
Human-readable file size (e.g., "245 MB").
.TP
.B Items
For directories: the count of files and subdirectories.
.TP
.B Perms
Standard Unix permission string (e.g., \fBdrwxr-xr-x\fR).
.TP
.B Owner / Group
The owning user and group.
.TP
.B Made / Mod / Acc
Creation, modification, and last access times.
.TP
.B Path
The absolute path to the item.
.SS Editing permissions and ownership
Press \fBTab\fR to move the keyboard into the panel; its border turns cyan and
a marker picks out the field the cursor is on. \fBj\fR and \fBk\fR walk the three
editable fields \(em permissions, owner, and group \(em and \fBEnter\fR opens a
dialog for the one under the cursor. \fBEsc\fR hands focus back to the tree
without hiding the panel.
.PP
Permissions are typed either octal (\fB644\fR, \fB0644\fR) or symbolic
(\fBrw\-r\-\-r\-\-\fR, with or without the leading type character, so what the
panel displays can be typed straight back). Owner and group take a name or a
numeric id. A value that cannot be read is refused before anything is changed,
rather than being guessed at.
.PP
The change applies to the tagged files, or to the file under the cursor when
nothing is tagged \(em the same rule \fBD\fR, \fBc\fR and \fBm\fR follow. When
exactly one directory is selected the dialog offers
.BR "Apply to everything inside" ,
which walks the subtree. The walk changes symlinks but never follows them, so
it cannot leave the tree; and it applies to a directory only after its
contents, so removing the \fBx\fR bit cannot cut off the rest of the walk.
.PP
This works on local and SFTP panels. Archives are read-only and refuse with a
notice.
.SS Width and height
\fB<\fR and \fB>\fR move the divider left and right in steps of five percent \(em
so \fB<\fR widens the panel and \fB>\fR narrows it \(em between
15% and 60% of the pane.
.PP
\fB+\fR and \fB\-\fR move the split between the metadata and the preview beneath
it, a row at a time: \fB+\fR grows the preview, \fB\-\fR gives the rows back.
Neither can squeeze the other out \(em the metadata keeps enough rows for the
fields that matter, and the preview keeps enough to be worth drawing.
.PP
The metadata is a fixed size, so by default everything past it goes to the
preview: a taller terminal makes the preview taller rather than spacing out the
same dozen fields. Both settings are remembered across directories and, on exit,
written to
.IR ~/.config/myd/prefs.toml .
.SS Preview
When the panel is wide and tall enough, the lower part of it shows the start of
the selected file, using the same reader as the full preview: highlighted text,
a rendered image, or a note saying why neither applies. It follows the cursor
and cannot be focused \(em it is a glance, and \fBspace\fR opens the full pane
for anything more.
.PP
An archive previews as its table of contents \(em member names alone here,
without the permission, size and timestamp columns the full pane shows, since
those are laid out before the name and in a box this narrow would be all that
fits. A file inside an archive previews like any other: members are read from a
file on this machine, so none of the remote handling applies to them. A
directory shows nothing, since there is nothing to read.
.PP
It declines the work that is too expensive to repeat on every cursor move:
remote images are not fetched, only the first few kilobytes of a text file are
read, and anything slow to open \(em an image, a PDF, an archive, or a file on
a server \(em waits for the cursor to settle before it starts.
.PP
In a split, each panel previews its own cursor's file and keeps doing so
whether or not it has focus \(em moving between panels changes which one is
outlined and nothing else.
.PP
An image here is drawn with the terminal's graphics protocol, at the same
resolution the full pane would give it, whenever this is the only such image on
screen. A split showing two info panels falls back to block characters for
both: the terminal is told about one image at a time, and on kitty removing
either would remove the other. Below roughly 20 columns or 16 rows the preview
yields and the metadata takes the whole panel; \fB<\fR is the answer when it
does not fit.
.PP
Hide or show the panel with \fBCtrl+p\fR.
.SH FILE PREVIEW
Press \fBspace\fR to open a pane over most of the screen showing what is in the
selected file, and \fBspace\fR again to close it. The pane follows the cursor:
hand focus back to the tree with \fBEsc\fR or \fBq\fR and moving the cursor
re-reads the preview, so a directory can be walked and its contents watched.
.PP
Text is syntax highlighted, using the file's extension or its \fB#!\fR line when
it has none. Highlighting is linear in the text and a 4300-line file costs about
a quarter of a second, so it is budgeted: the first 64 KB of a file is coloured
and the rest is shown plain. A long file therefore looks the same at the top
however long it is, rather than losing its colours entirely once it crosses a
threshold. Only the first megabyte of a large file is read; the footer reads
\fBtruncated\fR when that happened. A binary file is reported with its size
rather than written out as control characters.
.PP
Markdown has its own highlighter rather than going through the general grammar,
which makes it roughly four thousand times faster (37 microseconds against 170
milliseconds on a 30 KB file) and so exempt from that size limit. The general
grammar embeds every other language so fenced code can be highlighted in its own
syntax; that is far too slow for something which should feel instant. The trade is
that fenced code is coloured as one block rather than per language.
.PP
Images are drawn by
.BR timg (1)
or
.BR chafa (1),
whichever is on the \fBPATH\fR. \fBtimg\fR is preferred, as it renders more
formats. With neither installed the pane shows the file's details and says what is
missing.
.PP
How the image reaches the screen depends on the terminal. Three protocols carry
real pixel data: the kitty graphics protocol (kitty, ghostty, Konsole 22.04 and
later), iTerm2 inline images (iTerm2, WezTerm, mintty) and sixel (xterm built with
sixel, foot, contour, mlterm, Windows Terminal, iTerm2). Everywhere else the image
is approximated with Unicode block characters, which is universally safe but
visibly blocky, since a block packs four pixels into one cell and picks two
colours for them.
.PP
myd asks the terminal at start-up \(em a kitty graphics query and a Device
Attributes request, with a 100 millisecond budget \(em and falls back to
environment variables when there is nothing to ask or nothing answers. Sixel can
only be discovered this way; no environment variable reports it. For iTerm2 the
\fBLC_TERMINAL\fR variable is what identifies it, since \fBTERM_PROGRAM\fR is
not forwarded by \fBssh\fR and is overwritten by
.BR tmux (1).
.PP
Detection errs towards blocks deliberately: a terminal that does not understand a
graphics escape prints it, so a wrong guess sprays kilobytes of base64 over the
display. Set \fBMYD_PREVIEW_GRAPHICS\fR to \fBkitty\fR, \fBiterm2\fR,
\fBsixel\fR or \fBblocks\fR to force a choice.
.PP
Inside
.BR tmux (1)
the two mechanisms differ, which matters because the obvious setting only fixes
one of them. tmux parses and re-draws sixel itself, so it needs only to be told
the terminal supports it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set \-as terminal\-features ',*:sixel'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The kitty and iTerm2 protocols instead ride tmux's passthrough escape, which is
off by default:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set \-g allow\-passthrough on
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sixel is preferred inside tmux even though the others look better, because it
survives a multiplexer cleanly: it is a single escape sequence, where a kitty
image is a chain of dozens that each have to be wrapped individually. When myd
falls back to blocks anyway, the preview footer names the setting that would
change that.
.PP
Inside tmux the kitty protocol is used whatever the terminal's own protocol is.
This is about how the image is carried rather than what decodes it: kitty splits
a picture into roughly 4 KB chunks, each its own escape, while iTerm2 sends one
sequence however large \(em and a single large escape is what a multiplexer will
not deliver. iTerm2 understands kitty's protocol, so this costs nothing and
removes the size ceiling, keeping full resolution in tmux rather than shrinking
images to fit.
.PP
The per-escape limit remains as a backstop. \fBMYD_PREVIEW_MAX_PAYLOAD\fR sets
it in kilobytes, and \fB0\fR removes it. It is judged per sequence rather than
per image, which is the point of chunking.
.PP
Block output is captured and drawn as part of the interface rather than written to
the terminal, so it cannot corrupt the display. A graphics image necessarily does
reach the terminal directly: the pane leaves a hole for it and the escape is
written after the frame.
.PP
Because such an image is not made of cells, it is bounded explicitly. The kitty
and iTerm2 escapes are given the size in cells so the picture occupies exactly the
rows the pane reserved; left to itself the renderer describes the image in pixels
and the terminal decides how many rows that is, which is how an image spills past
its border. Sixel carries no such control and is asked for a smaller raster
instead.
.PP
myd works out how large a character cell is \(em from \fBTIOCGWINSZ\fR where the
kernel carries pixel dimensions, otherwise by asking the terminal
(\fBESC[16t\fR, answered in the same round trip as the protocol probe) \(em and
states the image's size in pixels. The kernel is preferred because under
.BR tmux (1)
it describes the pane, where the escape is answered by the outer terminal about
its whole window. The preview footer shows which cell size was used. Both pixels and cells are valid in the escape, but only one is
unambiguous: sizing in cells leaves the terminal to multiply out, and iTerm2
reaches a different answer inside
.BR tmux (1)
than it does natively. When no cell size is reported, the cell form is used
instead.
.PP
Because the size is stated separately from the image data, the raster itself is
requested at twice the size of the pane without the image occupying more of it. timg cannot
ask a piped stdout how large a cell is and assumes 18 pixels; on a display whose
cells are nearer 30 an image sized for that assumption arrives with too few
pixels and draws small and soft inside its box. Images are additionally rendered
with upscaling, or a small picture sits at its own pixel size in the middle of a
large pane. The oversampling is capped, since the payload grows with the square
of the raster.
.PP
Removing an image is protocol-specific too, and redrawing the screen is not
enough for any of them: ratatui writes only the cells whose content changed, and
the cells beneath an image are ones it believes are already blank. A kitty image
is an object the terminal re-composites and has a real delete operation; iTerm2
and sixel images belong to the cells they were drawn into and have none, so those
rows are erased explicitly and the following frame is a full repaint. This
happens when the preview closes, when the selection moves to another file, and on
exit.
.PP
A \fBPDF\fR needs a \fBtimg\fR linked against poppler; \fBchafa\fR has no PDF
loader, so on a machine with only \fBchafa\fR a PDF is reported as needing
\fBtimg\fR rather than failing obscurely. With a multi-page document open the
motions turn pages rather than scrolling, since a rendered page is drawn to fit and
has nothing to scroll, and the footer reads \fBpage 5/19\fR. The page count comes
from
.BR pdfinfo (1);
without poppler-utils installed you can still page forward, but there is no known
last page to jump to.
.PP
Keys below act on the pane while it has focus.
.TP
.B j, k
Scroll down or up one line. On a multi-page document they turn the page; on a
single image, which is drawn to fit and has nothing to scroll, they move the
tree's cursor instead and the preview follows to the next file. The arrow keys
do the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F, Ctrl+B
Scroll forward or back a full page, keeping one line of overlap. On a multi-page
document these turn a page.
.TP
.B Ctrl+D, Ctrl+U
Scroll half a page.
.TP
.B g, G
Jump to the start or the end \(em the first or last page of a multi-page document.
\fBHome\fR and \fBEnd\fR do the same.
.TP
.B /
Search within the file with a regular expression (case-insensitive). An empty
pattern clears the search; a malformed one is reported.
.TP
.B n, p
Step to the next or previous match, wrapping at the ends. \fBN\fR and \fBP\fR
step the other way. The current match is highlighted and the footer shows the
position, as in \fB3/11\fR.
.TP
.B q
Close the preview. Note this closes the pane rather than quitting \fBmyd\fR,
which is what \fBq\fR does elsewhere \(em reaching for it to dismiss a preview
should not end the session. It works whether or not the pane has focus.
.TP
.B Esc
Return focus to the tree, leaving the pane open so it follows the cursor.
.TP
.B Tab
Move focus in and out of the pane, as it does for the transfer sidebar.
.PP
On a remote panel the file is read from the server over SFTP, never from a
same-named path on the local machine. A remote image is fetched to a temporary
file first, capped at 32 MB, because both renderers take a path rather than a
stream.
.SH ARCHIVES
Press \fBspace\fR on an archive to list its contents, and \fBEnter\fR to browse
it as a filesystem.
.PP
The listing shows each member's permissions, size, timestamp and the path
\fIexactly as the archive stores it\fR \(em a member written as \fB./docs/x.md\fR
is shown that way rather than tidied up, because what is stored is what is being
asked about. A format that records no permissions shows \fB?---------\fR rather
than a guessed \fB0644\fR. The listing scrolls and \fB/\fR searches it, like any
other preview.
.PP
\fBEnter\fR opens the archive as a panel, and it behaves as a filesystem from
there: \fBt\fR tags, \fBf\fR filters, \fB/\fR searches, \fBs\fR sorts, \fBv\fR
switches to the treemap, and \fBspace\fR previews a file inside it. Directory
sizes are true recursive totals \(em unlike a remote panel, an archive knows
every member's size already, so the size bars and the treemap are meaningful.
.PP
Extracting is the ordinary copy: \fBc\fR into the other pane in a split, or
\fBc\fR and a destination in a single pane. Directories extract recursively, and
the work rides the transfer queue, so a large extract shows progress and leaves
the interface responsive.
.PP
Archives are read\-only. \fBD\fR, \fBR\fR, \fBN\fR and \fBm\fR refuse with a
message rather than appearing to work. The pane says so in its title, which
names the archive and is tinted amber\-brown, and in a read\-only badge in the
footer.
.SS Formats
Read in process, with no external tools: \fBzip\fR (and \fB.jar\fR, \fB.apk\fR,
\fB.whl\fR, \fB.xpi\fR, and the rest of the family), \fBtar\fR and its
compressed forms (\fB.tar.gz\fR/\fB.tgz\fR, \fB.tar.bz2\fR, \fB.tar.xz\fR,
\fB.tar.zst\fR), single compressed files (\fB.gz\fR, \fB.bz2\fR, \fB.xz\fR,
\fB.zst\fR, shown as the one member they hold), and \fB7z\fR.
.PP
Comic book archives are the containers they are wearing another name, and open
as such: \fB.cbz\fR is a zip, \fB.cbr\fR a rar, \fB.cbt\fR a tar and \fB.cb7\fR
a 7z. \fBspace\fR lists the pages and \fBEnter\fR opens the archive to page
through them with the preview.
.PP
\fBRAR\fR is listed in process by the \fBrars\fR crate, which is pure Rust and
handles all three RAR generations including solid archives, so browsing one
needs nothing installed. Reading a member out of one prefers \fBbsdtar\fR(1)
where it is present: \fBrars\fR cannot seek to a member and decodes everything
ahead of it, which made stepping through a large archive with the preview open
cost tens of seconds per file. Without \fBbsdtar\fR it still works, at that
cost for members late in the archive.
.PP
\fB.iso\fR, \fB.cab\fR, \fB.cpio\fR, \fB.lha\fR, \fB.xar\fR, \fB.deb\fR and
\fB.rpm\fR are read through \fBbsdtar\fR(1) (libarchive) when it is on the
\fBPATH\fR \(em the long tail nobody would write a reader for individually.
Without \fBbsdtar\fR these report what is missing rather than failing obscurely,
the same arrangement as \fBtimg\fR/\fBchafa\fR for images.
.PP
Office formats are deliberately not treated as archives. \fB.docx\fR and
\fB.odt\fR are zips, but a table of \fBword/document.xml\fR entries is less
useful than the preview they already get.
.SS Limits
An archive on a remote panel is refused: opening one would mean downloading it
whole, which should be a copy requested explicitly and shown progressing. An
archive inside another archive is refused for browsing, though \fBspace\fR still
lists it \(em the cost multiplies through a stream\-compressed outer archive, and
the nesting depth is the archive's choice rather than the user's.
.PP
There is no limit on the size of a local container. It is memory\-mapped rather
than read, so its size costs address space rather than memory: indexing a zip
touches its tail, indexing a tar walks the headers and skips the data, and
neither faults in the bulk of the file. A stored member \(em every member of a
plain tar, and any zip entry written uncompressed \(em is a contiguous run of
bytes in the container and is read straight out of the mapping, so it extracts
in constant memory however large it is.
.PP
A compressed tar cannot be mapped: it has no directory, so it has to be
decompressed in full before anything can be indexed. That decompression fills a
buffer sized from the memory actually available (a quarter of it, honouring a
cgroup cap where there is one) and spills the remainder to a temporary file,
which is mapped and unlinked. Memory therefore stays bounded whatever the stream
expands to \(em a decompression bomb costs disk the system reclaims when the
archive is closed rather than the machine's RAM.
.PP
A container on a remote panel has nothing to map, so listing one means pulling it
across the wire; past 128 MB that is refused with a note suggesting it be copied
across first. Names that escape the archive root ("Zip Slip") are refused at
index time and reported in the listing rather than dropped silently. The number
of members is still bounded \(em 100,000 browsing, 20,000 in a preview \(em since
the index holds a record per member; that is a count rather than a size.
.PP
Extraction does not restore the executable bit: a \fBchmod +x\fR script comes out
of an archive non\-executable. The mode is held in the index and shown in the
listing, but passing it through would mean widening the write path for every
backend.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B myd
Start browsing in the current directory.
.TP
.B myd \-d
Start on the picker and choose a destination from the saved list.
.TP
.B myd ~/Documents
Start browsing at \fB~/Documents\fR.
.TP
.B myd /var/log
Start browsing at \fB/var/log\fR.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.TP
.B Rust
1.75 or later (stable).
.TP
.B Terminal
A terminal with true color (256-color or 24-bit) support.
.TP
.B timg or chafa
Optional, for image previews in the preview pane. Neither is required; without
them the pane shows the file's details instead. PDF previews need a
.BR timg (1)
built with poppler, which \fBtimg \-\-version\fR reports.
.TP
.B pdfinfo
Optional, from poppler-utils, so the preview pane knows how many pages a PDF has.
.TP
.B A graphics-capable terminal
Optional, for pixel-resolution images rather than block approximations: one
implementing the kitty graphics protocol, iTerm2 inline images, or sixel. Under
.BR tmux (1)
see the tmux settings described under
.BR "FILE PREVIEW" ;
sixel and the other two protocols need different ones.
.SH INSTALLATION
.PP
.RS
.nf
cargo install \-\-path .
.fi
.RE
.PP
Or run directly without installation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cargo run
cargo run \-\- ~/Documents
.fi
.RE
